<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mtamvuna River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Umtamvuna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mtamvuna is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> that flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and forms part of the boundary between the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>. In the 19th century it marked the north-eastern edge of Pondoland, separating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpondo">Mpondo</a> kingdom from Natal. Its gorge is protected as a nature reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mtamvuna-river.jpg" alt="Mtamvuna River" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris&amp;Kerri · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umtamvuna_River_Gorge.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
